{{nihongo foot|'''''Super Mario 64'''''|スーパーマリオ64|Sūpā Mario 64|lead=yes|group=lower-alpha}} is a 1996 [[Platformer|platform game]] developed and published by [[Nintendo]] for the [[Nintendo 64]]. It is the first ''[[Super Mario]]'' game to feature [[3D graphics]], combining traditional ''Super Mario'' gameplay, visual style, and characters in a large [[open world]]. In the game, [[Bowser]] invades [[Princess Peach]]'s castle, kidnaps her, and hides the castle's Power Stars in different worlds inside magical paintings. As [[Mario]], the player traverses levels and collects Power Stars to unlock areas of the castle, reach Bowser and rescue Peach.

Director [[Shigeru Miyamoto]] conceived a 3D ''Super Mario'' game during the production of ''[[Star Fox (1993 video game)|Star Fox]]'' (1993). The team spent about one year on design and twenty months on production, starting with the [[virtual camera system]]. The team continued with illustrating the 3D character models and refining [[Sprite (computer graphics)|sprite]] movements. Yoji Inagaki recorded the sound effects, and the score was composed by [[Koji Kondo]].

''Super Mario 64'' was highly anticipated, boosted by advertising campaigns and showings at the 1996{{nbsp}}[[E3]] trade show. It was released in Japan and North America in 1996 and [[PAL region]]s in 1997 to acclaim, with reviewers praising its ambition, visuals, level design, and gameplay; however, some criticized its camera system. It is the [[List of best-selling Nintendo 64 video games|best-selling Nintendo 64 game]], with nearly twelve{{nbsp}}million copies sold by 2015.

''Super Mario 64'' is considered as one of the [[List of video games considered the best|greatest and most influential video games]] of all time. Numerous developers have cited it as an influence on [[3D platform game]]s, with its camera system and 360-degree [[analog control]] establishing a new archetype, much as ''[[Super Mario Bros.]]'' did for [[side-scrolling]] platform games. Its influence can be seen in 3D platformers such as ''[[Spyro the Dragon]]'', ''[[Banjo-Kazooie (video game)|Banjo-Kazooie]]'', and ''[[Psychonauts]]''. It was remade as ''[[Super Mario 64 DS]]'' for the [[Nintendo DS]] in 2004, and has been [[Video game port|ported]] to other [[Nintendo video game consoles|Nintendo consoles]]. The game has produced many [[Fan game|fangames]] and [[Video game modding|mods]], a large [[speedrunning]] community, and enduring rumors about its features and development.
