'''''Donkey Kong 64''''' is a 1999 [[platform game]] developed by [[Rare (company)|Rare]] and published by [[Nintendo]] for the [[Nintendo 64]]. It is the first ''[[Donkey Kong]]'' game to feature 3D gameplay. As the gorilla [[Donkey Kong (character)|Donkey Kong]], the player explores themed [[Level (video games)|levels]] to collect items and rescue his family members from the crocodile [[King K. Rool]]. The player completes [[minigame]]s and puzzles as five playable [[List of Donkey Kong characters|Kong characters]]—each with their own special abilities—to receive bananas and other collectibles. In [[multiplayer]] modes, up to four players can compete in [[Deathmatch (video games)|deathmatch]] and [[last man standing (video games)|last man standing]] games.

Rare began working on ''Donkey Kong 64'' in 1997, following the completion of ''[[Donkey Kong Country 3]]'' (1996). A 16-member team, with many staff from Rare's ''[[Banjo-Kazooie (video game)|Banjo-Kazooie]]'' (1998) team, developed it over three years. It was conceived as a [[2.5D]] platformer similar to ''Country'' before it was reworked into a more open-ended game using the ''Banjo'' [[game engine|engine]]. Rare sought to differentiate ''Donkey Kong 64'' from ''Banjo'' with more collectibles and player characters, cinematic sequences, and bombastic [[Boss (video games)|bosses]]. It was the first of two games to require the Nintendo 64 [[Expansion Pak]], an accessory that added [[random-access memory|memory]] resources. [[Grant Kirkhope]] composed the soundtrack, which includes a [[comedy hip-hop]] song, the "[[DK Rap]]", that plays in the introduction.

''Donkey Kong 64'' was released in North America in November 1999 and worldwide in December. Nintendo backed the release with a {{USD|22 million}} marketing campaign that included advertisements, [[sweepstake]]s, and a national tour. ''Donkey Kong 64'' received acclaim and was Nintendo's bestseller during the 1999 [[holiday season]], selling 5.27 million copies worldwide by 2021. It won the 1999 [[Game Critics Awards|E3 Game Critics award]] for Best Platform Game and multiple awards and nominations from magazines. Reviewers praised its visuals, size, and length, but criticized its [[Virtual camera system|camera]] and emphasis on item collection and backtracking. Some cited its gameplay and visual similarities to ''Banjo-Kazooie'' as a detriment.

''Donkey Kong 64'' was Rare's final ''Donkey Kong'' game before its acquisition by [[Microsoft]] in 2002, the last major ''Donkey Kong'' game until ''[[Donkey Kong Jungle Beat]]'' (2004), and the franchise's only 3D platformer until ''[[Donkey Kong Bananza]]'' (2025). Nintendo rereleased it via the [[Wii U]]'s [[Virtual Console]] in 2015 and the [[Nintendo Classics]] service in 2026. Retrospective reviews were mixed; critics considered it emblematic of the tedium in Rare's "collect-a-thon" adventure platformers for its excessive emphasis on collecting items and have blamed it for precipitating 3D platforming's decline in popularity. [[Video game journalist]]s have called the "DK Rap" one of the worst songs in a video game,{{r|DK Rap}}<!-- per talk page, this statement is often challenged and thus warrants a lede citation (see MOS:CITELEAD) --> and it has garnered popularity as an [[internet meme]].

