{{nihongo foot|'''''PaRappa the Rapper'''''|パラッパラッパー|Parappa Rappā|lead=yes|group=lower-alpha}}  is a [[rhythm video game]] developed by [[NanaOn-Sha]] and published by [[Sony Computer Entertainment]] for the [[PlayStation (console)|PlayStation]]. It was originally released in Japan in 1996 and worldwide in 1997. Created by music producer [[Masaya Matsuura]] in collaboration with artist [[Rodney Alan Greenblat|Rodney Greenblat]], the game features unique visual design and [[rapping|rap]]-based gameplay and is considered to be the first true rhythm game.<ref name="Music">{{cite web |last1=Davis |first1=Justin |title=The History of Music Games |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2014/10/21/the-history-of-music-games |publisher=[[IGN]] |access-date=18 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180531220623/https://www.ign.com/articles/2014/10/21/the-history-of-music-games |archive-date=31 May 2018 |date=21 October 2014}}</ref> It was ported to the [[PlayStation Portable]] in 2006 in celebration of its 10-year anniversary. A remastered version of the original PlayStation game was released for [[PlayStation 4]] in 2017 for the game's twentieth anniversary.

''PaRappa the Rapper'' was acclaimed by critics, who praised its music, story, animation, and gameplay, though its short length was criticized. Several publications list it as one of [[List of video games considered the best|the best video games ever made]]. It spawned two follow-up titles; a [[electric guitar|guitar]]-based spin-off titled ''[[Um Jammer Lammy]]'', released in 1999 for the PlayStation, and a direct sequel, ''[[PaRappa the Rapper 2]]'', released for the [[PlayStation 2]] in 2001. The game was also influential in setting the basic template for the rhythm game genre, including franchises such as the [[Bemani]] and [[Harmonix]] games.

